    {
      "id": "pipeline-effect",
      "title": "Pipeline effect",
      "deck": "SET/GET mix (1:10 ratio) with 100-byte values, comparing pipeline depth 1 vs 30. Candidate statistic: pipeline-30 throughput.",
      "unit": "ops/s",
      "lowerIsBetter": false,
      "verdict": {
        "winnerId": "kividb",
        "headline": "KiviDB 22.2M ops/s at pipeline 30, a floor with the client at 69% CPU; Dragonfly 16.7M; Redis 1.48M; Valkey 1.37M",
        "summary": "SET/GET mix (1:10) with 100-byte values at pipeline depth 30, 200 connections. KiviDB's slowest pass, 21.5M ops/s, beat Dragonfly's fastest, 16.8M, and its median led by 33%. That number is a floor: the client averaged 69% CPU in KiviDB's cell (66 to 70% per pass), 50 memtier threads can keep at most 50 of its 64 vCPUs busy, and KiviDB's passes got slower as client CPU rose (r = −0.95 over ten passes). Dragonfly's cell ran the client at 34% with no such pattern. Pipelining raised throughput 15.2 times for KiviDB, 11.9 for Dragonfly, 8.2 for Redis and 7.6 for Valkey, and Redis led Valkey by 8%."
      },
